WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.567
This service counts retries to the card issuer.

2
00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:08.495
The counter field is volatile, so it looks safe.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.703
Four threads each add one, a million times.

4
00:00:11.703 --> 00:00:13.449
What total do you expect?

5
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:17.771
One measured run ended near 3.7 million.

6
00:00:17.771 --> 00:00:22.151
Over three hundred thousand retries vanished, and nothing threw.

7
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:24.827
First, take volatile away for a moment.

8
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:32.624
With a plain field, the JIT may keep a copy in a register, and thread 2 reads that copy again and again.

9
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:34.352
Thread 1 writes 42.

10
00:00:34.352 --> 00:00:36.431
Thread 2 may never see it.

11
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.925
Put volatile back, and replay.

12
00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:43.719
Every read now goes back to the field, so thread 2's next read sees 42.

13
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:46.747
And the write carries what came before it.

14
00:00:46.747 --> 00:00:51.904
Plain writes thread 1 made before setting 42 are visible to whoever reads 42.

15
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:54.932
So the field is always fresh.

16
00:00:54.932 --> 00:00:57.175
Now both threads run retries++.

17
00:00:57.175 --> 00:01:00.213
That is three steps: read, add one, write.

18
00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:02.533
Thread 1 reads 41.

19
00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:04.488
A fresh volatile read.

20
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.640
Thread 2 reads too.

21
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.681
Also fresh, also 41.

22
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.357
Both add one.

23
00:01:10.357 --> 00:01:11.815
Both hold 42.

24
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:14.725
Thread 1 writes 42, visible at once.

25
00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:16.221
Now thread 2 writes.

26
00:01:16.221 --> 00:01:18.091
What does the field hold?

27
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:19.951
42.

28
00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:22.987
Two increments, and one is lost.

29
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.879
Every access was a correct volatile access.

30
00:01:25.879 --> 00:01:30.552
volatile made each one visible, and did nothing about the gap between them.

31
00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:35.127
The fix is one step that reads and writes together.

32
00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:38.129
AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet is that step.

33
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:42.234
Thread 2 goes before it or after it, never inside.

34
00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:43.872
The count ends at 43.

35
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:45.865
So which tool?

36
00:01:45.865 --> 00:01:51.932
One writer, and readers want the latest value: volatile is enough, like a shutdown flag.

37
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:56.759
Many writers updating one variable from its old value: an atomic.

38
00:01:56.759 --> 00:01:59.464
Several fields under one rule: a lock.

39
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:04.533
In code, that is an AtomicInteger, in Java and in Kotlin.

40
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:09.019
The rule: volatile makes each read and write visible and ordered.

41
00:02:09.019 --> 00:02:11.244
It never joins them into one step.

42
00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:13.293
Quick check.

43
00:02:13.293 --> 00:02:18.924
Many threads run max = Math.max(max, x) on a volatile field.

44
00:02:18.924 --> 00:02:20.144
Is that safe?

45
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:22.565
No.

46
00:02:22.565 --> 00:02:26.435
It reads, decides, then writes, with the same gap.

47
00:02:26.435 --> 00:02:28.369
Use an atomic, or a lock.
